.TH CFAB 8 "September 2026" "cfab 0.1.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
cfab \- per-host runtime of the resilient converged fabric for small clusters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cfab
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIPATH\fR]
[\fB\-\-host\fR \fIMEMBER\fR]
.I subcommand
[\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cfab
applies, verifies, and removes the converged network fabric on one cluster
member. The fabric is declared once, in
.BR fabric.toml ;
everything else \(em segment sub-interfaces, the nft forward policy and
traffic-class marking, per-NIC HTB shaping trees, and the FRR
(OSPF/BFD/BGP) configuration \(em is derived from that declaration and
generated by this binary. Physical NICs are transport; traffic classes
(storage, cluster, management) are policy layered above them: connectivity is
additive, failover automatic, and control traffic protected under saturation.
.PP
Both member kinds need
.BR ip (8).
A
.B host
needs
.BR nft (8):
it installs
.BR "table inet cfab" ,
which carries the per-zone bulk DSCP clamp and the fallback control-egress
ceiling, and a kernel without nf_tables is a hard refusal for that kind. A
.B leaf
takes
.BR nft (8)
too where the kernel has it, and otherwise falls back to
.BR iptables-legacy (8),
.BR iptables-legacy-save (8)
and
.BR iptables-legacy-restore (8),
installing the ceiling ALONE \(em that kernel has no DSCP target, so the
per-zone bulk clamp is skipped and
.B cfab status
names the backend and says so. The choice is made at
.B up
on the kernel's own refusal and recorded in the run dir. This member's own
OSPF and BFD are marked by the engine itself (DSCP CS6, and skb-priority
PCP_CTRL onto the wire through the segment sub-interface's egress-qos-map),
not by that table, which lets them past the clamp untouched. A
.B host
additionally needs
.BR tc (8)
and
.BR ethtool (8)
for its shaping trees and per-NIC offload posture; a
.B leaf
shapes nothing \(em its wires' qdiscs belong to its own OS. A missing tool is
refused by name before anything is applied.
.PP
Most subcommands require root. The generators
.RB ( "cfab gen" ,
.BR "cfab check" ,
.BR "cfab schema" )
are pure: they read the declaration and change nothing.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B check
Parse and validate
.BR fabric.toml ;
print the resolved view for this member (zones, segments, wires, ingress
legs). Validation fails loudly on any inconsistency; an unknown key in the
declaration is a parse error naming its line and column.
.TP
.B schema
Print the
.B fabric.toml
declaration schema (the file an operator writes, not cfab's internal model) as
JSON Schema. Needs no config file.
.TP
.BR "gen policy" | "mark [--backend nft|iptables-legacy]" | engine
Print a generated artifact: the nft forward policy (table inet cfab-fwd:
default-deny for anything touching a cfab interface, declared pairs pass, the
admin NIC never transits, packets touching no cfab interface are accepted as
another stack's business), the traffic-class marking (table inet cfab: the
per-zone bulk DSCP clamp plus one derived control-egress ceiling per fallback
bond \(em or, with
.BR --backend " iptables-legacy" ,
the ceiling alone as an
.BR iptables-legacy-restore (8)
input, which is what a leaf installs on a kernel without nf_tables), or
this member's routing-engine configuration tree (JSON).
.TP
.BI "gen shape " DEV " " [\fB\-\-tc\fR|\fB\-\-expect\fR]
Print the floor+borrow HTB derivation for one physical NIC.
.B \-\-tc
prints the tc program instead;
.B \-\-expect
prints the "classid effective-rate" lines
.B status
diffs against the live qdisc tree.
.TP
.B run
Apply the fabric on this member \(em segment sub-interfaces, policy, marking,
shaping, and routing-engine config \(em then stay in the foreground and
supervise the engine, the shape daemon, and (only when pmxcfs reports a
cluster) conf-sync as direct child processes. This is the long-lived
.B "systemd Type=notify"
service and the container PID 1; it is not run by hand. The apply is
idempotent.
.B SIGHUP
re\-reads the declaration and acts on it (see
.B RELOAD
below), while
.B SIGTERM
runs the stop sequence: forwarding off first, then each child is signalled and
given 10 s before SIGKILL, then the fabric is torn down. Only one supervisor
runs at a time, guarded by a lock on the run dir.
.TP
.B down
Remove everything cfab applied and restore FRR to its pre-fabric state. Never
touches interfaces or firewall state the fabric does not own. Refuses while a
supervisor is still running.
.TP
.BR status " [\fB\-\-wait\fR \fIN\fR] [\fB\-\-permissive\fR]"
What this member's fabric is doing right now, in one of four states:
.BR UP ", " UP\-DEGRADED ", " FAILED " or " DOWN
(exit 0, 1, 2, 3). The headline carries three fixed counts,
.RI ( peers " | " links " | " fallbacks ),
each
.IR n / N :
.B peers
= peers with at least one available adjacency (self is never counted);
.B links
= BFD sessions, one per (peer, zone, segment) the two members share;
.B fallbacks
= OSPF neighbors on the fallback segment, one per peer carrying that zone's
row, available at 2\-Way or better. All three denominators come from
.BR fabric.toml ,
so a cable pull moves the numerator and never the denominator. A member
declaring no fallback row prints
.BR 0/0 ,
never a missing field.
.IP
One indented line per condition follows the headline, sorted, each condition
named once.
.B status
never writes: a condition that makes a link unsafe is brought down by
.BR "cfab fwd\-watchdog" ,
and the state then follows from the counts; everything else is a reason line
that does not move the state.
.IP
.B status
describes the fabric that is
.IR running ,
not the file: the supervisor keeps the declaration it applied at
.IR RUN_DIR/fabric.toml.applied ,
and
.B status
prefers that copy, so an operator editing
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml
\(em or one whose bad edit
.B reload
has just refused \(em still gets the state of the fabric instead of a parse
error. Every other subcommand keeps reading the file: they act on what is
declared. When the file on disk disagrees with the running fabric,
.B status
adds one reason line and the state is unchanged. An unreadable, unparseable or
invalid file \(em including one that no longer declares this member \(em reads
.IP
.nf
declaration /etc/cfab/fabric.toml: \fIerror\fP (status describes the running
fabric; a reload of this file will be refused)
.fi
.IP
with the parser's own line, column and snippet, its continuation lines
indented; a valid file that differs from what is running reads
.IP
.nf
declaration /etc/cfab/fabric.toml changed since apply (systemctl reload cfab
to apply; the fabric will restart)
.fi
.IP
Equality is semantic, so a comment or whitespace edit is not a change. The
copy lives with the fabric:
.B down
removes the run dir whole and takes it along.
.IP
.B \-\-wait
.I N
re\-reads every 2\ s until the state is UP or
.I N
seconds pass, then reports whatever state was reached \(em there is no
"not converged" verdict.
.B DOWN
ends the wait at once (there is no intent to wait for);
.B FAILED
and
.B UP\-DEGRADED
do not, because a fabric coming up passes through both. Default
.BR "\-\-wait 0" ,
one instant read.
.B \-\-permissive
exits 0 for
.B UP\-DEGRADED
as well as
.BR UP ;
it never masks
.B FAILED
or
.BR DOWN .
.IP
.B Bootstrap:
the first member of a fresh fabric has no peer to be adjacent to, so it
honestly reads
.BR "FAILED (0/P | 0/L | 0/F)" ,
and
.B \-\-permissive
does not hide it. The second member turns both UP.
.TP
.BI "measure\-cap " "DEV PEER " [ SECS ]
Flood
.I PEER
on
.I DEV
for
.I SECS
seconds (default 6) with an unmarked native UDP flood and record the wire's
measured capacity to the cap file the shape derivation reads. On a clustered
member the flood runs under the
.I priv/lock/cfab\-measure
cluster lease (one measurement at a time cluster-wide; if another member holds
it, the run refuses and says so \(em retry shortly), and the measured cap is
also published to
.IR /etc/pve/cfab/caps/MEMBER/cap\-DEV ,
where the shape derivation finds it again should the local (tmpfs) cap file be
lost; a publication failure is a warning, never a measurement failure. Refuses
to run while the fabric's shaper holds
.IR DEV 's
egress qdisc \(em the shape-daemon child of
.B cfab run
holds it while the fabric is up, and the measurement needs the qdisc free.
.TP
.B policy\-teeth
Prove the forward policy in throwaway network namespaces \(em every zone pair
exercised against the declared allow-list \(em and then prove the proof
bites, by regressing the policy in memory and requiring the matrix to go red.
Cleans up its namespaces even on failure.
.TP
.B shape\-daemon
Membership-reactive shaping loop; a supervised child of
.B cfab run
on host members. Not intended for interactive use.
.TP
.B fwd\-watchdog
One fail-closed forwarding-posture check. Inside
.B cfab run
this runs as an in-process task on a few-second tick (it replaces a systemd
timer); the subcommand runs a single check by hand.
If the forward policy is not loaded with a drop default, it disables
forwarding on every cfab interface and exits nonzero. A cfab interface whose
forwarding flag drifted from the declared value (a foreign stack writing
.I ip_forward=1
propagates onto every interface) is written back and logged. Interfaces cfab
does not own are never read or written: another forwarder on the host (Docker,
a routed bridge, a host-level CNI) is not cfab's to police.
.TP
.B cluster status
Report the pmxcfs coordination state: presence, quorum (the
.I /etc/pve/local
owner-write-bit probe), the member list with online flags, the published
.B fabric.toml
(generation, size, first line), and the published measured caps (one line per
member). When
.I /etc/pve
is absent or pmxcfs runs in local mode, prints a clean \(lqnot clustered\(rq
report and exits 0 \(em coordination is additive, never a dependency.
.TP
.B conf publish
Validate the local
.B fabric.toml
fully (a declaration that does not validate is refused, never published),
require a quorate cluster, then publish it cluster-wide: take the
.I priv/lock/cfab\-conf
cluster lock (with stale-lock recovery after the 120\ s pmxcfs expiry), write
.I /etc/pve/cfab/fabric.toml
atomically (temp file + rename), bump
.IR /etc/pve/cfab/gen ,
and release the lock. Ack directories older than the previous generation are
then removed (best effort).
.TP
.B conf\-sync
Cluster config-sync daemon; a supervised child of
.B cfab run
on every clustered member (publisher included \(em it is not special). Not
intended for interactive use. Every second it checks quorum (non-quorate:
do nothing, reads may be stale) and the published generation. On a new
generation it validates the published conf with the full gate (an invalid
publish is refused loudly and never applied, and that generation is never
retried), caches the current
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml
to
.IR fabric.toml.prev ,
installs the published text, asks the supervisor to re-apply it over
.BR cfab.sock ,
and runs
.B status
(exit 0 or 1 \(em UP or UP\-DEGRADED \(em counts as carrying traffic). It then
writes its ack file and
waits up to 60\ s for an ack from at least one
.I different
member. No peer ack, a
.B status
of FAILED or DOWN, or a failed ack write (the severed\-node
signal: a member that cannot write acks cannot see them either) \(em all
REVERT to the previous conf and re-apply it.
A byte-identical publish acks and commits without applying. A single-member
cluster skips the witness wait.
.IP
.B Note (2-node clusters):
a lone applier whose peer is down reverts after the 60\ s window \(em by
design, the fail-safe refuses an unwitnessed conf; re-publish once the peer
is back.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c ", " \-\-config " PATH"
Path to
.BR fabric.toml .
Default search order: a
.B fabric.toml
beside the binary, then
.IR /etc/cfab/fabric.toml ,
then
.B ./fabric.toml
in the working directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-host " MEMBER"
The `[[member]]` row to run as. Default:
.BR $CFAB_HOST ,
else the kernel hostname.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help ", " \-V ", " \-\-version
Standard help and version output.
.SH RELOAD
.B "systemctl reload cfab"
(SIGHUP, or a
.B reapply
request on the supervisor socket \(em one code path) re\-reads
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml
and compares the fabric it derives with the running one. Comments and whitespace do
not count: the comparison is of the derived fabric, not the file bytes. Reloads never
overlap: one arriving during another queues behind it.
.TP
.B unchanged
Re\-applied in place: no teardown, no netdev churn, but the engine and the shape
daemon restart \(em measured 2026\-09\-06 on the testbed, 6\(en10 s of loss on every
zone while OSPF re\-converges.
.TP
.B "changed and valid"
The fabric is torn down and the unit restarted onto the new declaration (exit 6). An
in\-place apply cannot reach it: the apply creates and repairs, it never removes what
the previous declaration had. The whole fabric is down for the restart, as on a
package upgrade.
.TP
.B "invalid, unreadable, or no longer declaring this host"
Refused. The running fabric is kept exactly as it was, and
.B "cfab status"
names the refusal on its
.B components:
line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B status
maps its state to the exit code, Nagios\-style (0/1/2/3).
.B run
uses 0/1/3/4/6: 0 on a clean stop, 1 on a runtime failure, 3 when the apply is
refused, 4 when another supervisor already holds the lock, 6 when a reload found a
changed declaration. 3 and 4 are the unit's
.BR RestartPreventExitStatus ,
neither fixed by trying again; 6 is its
.B SuccessExitStatus
and
.BR RestartForceExitStatus .
Every other subcommand uses 0 for success and 1 for failure.
.TP
.B 0
.B status
.BR UP :
every expected adjacency available. For
.BR run ,
a clean stop \(em the SIGTERM teardown completed. For every other subcommand,
success.
.TP
.B 1
.B status
.BR UP\-DEGRADED :
up and carrying traffic, but some expected adjacency is down (a wire pulled, a
session not yet established) \(em 0 under
.BR \-\-permissive .
For
.BR run ,
a runtime failure that systemd retries after
.BR RestartSec .
For every other subcommand, failure: a validation error, a failed apply, a
bitten watchdog, or
.B policy\-teeth
finding the policy (or its own teeth) broken. A
.B fabric.toml
that is present but unparseable also exits 1, from any subcommand
.B status
included \(em we cannot know what was desired.
.TP
.B 2
.B status
.BR FAILED :
no adjacency is available at all while up is desired.
.B \-\-permissive
does not mask it.
.B run
does not use this code.
.TP
.B 3
.B status
.BR DOWN :
up is not desired: there is no run dir \(em
.B cfab down
was run, or
.B cfab run
never was \(em or there is no
.B fabric.toml
on this host at all.
.B \-\-permissive
does not mask it. For
.BR run ,
the declaration or this host refused the apply; retrying will not help.
.TP
.B 4
.B run
only: another supervisor already holds the lock on the run dir. Retrying will
not help.
.TP
.B 6
.B run
only: a reload found a changed declaration. The stop sequence completed \(em this is
a clean stop, not a failure \(em and the unit is restarted to apply the new file. Note
that 5 belongs to a supervised child that could not arm its parent\-death signal, not
to this.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CFAB_HOST
Member name to run as when
.B \-\-host
is not given.
.TP
.B CFAB_UP_IFS
Space-separated authoritative up-set for
.BR "gen shape" ;
overrides sysfs carrier detection.
.TP
.B CFAB_CAP_DIR
Directory holding
.BI cap\- DEV
measured-capacity files (default: the declaration's run directory).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml
Site declaration (not shipped; an example is in
.IR /usr/share/doc/cfab/examples/ ).
On a clustered member,
.B conf\-sync
maintains it as the last-known-good cluster conf.
.TP
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml.prev
The
.B conf\-sync
revert target: the conf that was live before the last cluster apply.
.TP
.I RUN_DIR/fabric.toml.applied
The declaration the supervisor applied, written before its initial apply and
removed with the run dir by
.BR down .
.B status
reads it so it can describe the running fabric while
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml
is mid\-edit or refused. Found through the
.B run_dir
the file declares, else the packaged default; a member that both moved
.B run_dir
and has an unparseable file leaves nothing on disk to say where the copy is, and
.B status
then fails on the parse error as before. Never re\-read by anything else.
.TP
.I RUN_DIR/wire\-drivers, RUN_DIR/wire\-driver\-features
Written by
.BR up ,
one line each.
.I wire\-drivers
records the
.BR "ethtool \-i" " driver every present wire had at apply; the forwarding"
watchdog compares it when a wire's netdev returns and reports an adapter that
is not the one that was configured.
.I wire\-driver\-features
records
.RI "\(dq" "wire feature prior" "\(dq for every feature"
.B up
actually changed from a
.B driver_features
string, and
.B down
replays each line as
.BR "ethtool \-K" .
.TP
.I RUN_DIR/conf\-sync\-attempted, RUN_DIR/conf\-sync\-committed
The daemon's generation state (decimal), under the declaration's run
directory.
.B attempted
is written before any apply, so a refused or reverted generation is never
retried, even across a daemon restart.
.TP
.I /etc/iproute2/rt_protos.d/cfab.conf
Names the engine's private kernel route\-protocol ids for
.B ip route
output only (
.BR cfab\-ospf ", " cfab\-static ", " cfab\-bgp ", " cfab\-other ,
201\-204); cosmetic, cfab's own sweep matches the numeric ids.
.TP
.I /etc/pve/cfab/fabric.toml
The cluster-published declaration (pmxcfs; written by
.BR "conf publish" ).
.TP
.I /etc/pve/cfab/gen
Publication generation counter (decimal; absent means 0).
.TP
.I /etc/pve/cfab/acks/GEN/MEMBER
Per-member apply acknowledgments for generation
.IR GEN .
.TP
.I /etc/pve/cfab/caps/MEMBER/cap\-DEV
Cluster-published measured capacities (written by
.BR measure\-cap ;
the shape derivation's fallback when the local cap file is absent, cached back
to it on first use).
.TP
.IR /etc/pve/priv/lock/cfab\-conf ", " /etc/pve/priv/lock/cfab\-measure
The publish cluster lock and the measurement lease (mkdir locks; pmxcfs
expires a crashed holder's lock after 120\ s).
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/cfab.service
The packaged systemd unit (see
.BR SYSTEMD ).
.TP
.I /etc/default/cfab
Optional environment file for that unit (a conffile): where to set
.B CFAB_HOST
when this member's row is not named by the kernel hostname.
.SH SYSTEMD
The Debian package ships
.BR cfab.service ,
installed
.B disabled and not started
\(em installing cfab never changes the network. Write
.I /etc/cfab/fabric.toml
first, then
.BR "systemctl enable \-\-now cfab" .
.PP
The unit is
.BR Type=notify .
.B ExecStart
is
.BR "cfab run" ,
the long-lived supervisor.
.B ExecReload
is
.BR "kill \-HUP $MAINPID" ,
which re\-reads the declaration (see
.B RELOAD
below).
.B ConditionPathExists=/etc/cfab/fabric.toml
skips it on a host that has the package but no declaration, so that host boots clean
instead of failing.
.B RestartPreventExitStatus=3\ 4
stops systemd from retrying a refused apply or a lost lock, while
.B SuccessExitStatus=6
and
.B RestartForceExitStatus=6
turn a changed\-declaration reload into a clean restart rather than a failure.
.PP
A package upgrade restarts the unit once the new files are in place (teardown and
re\-apply: every identity on the host is down for the restart). It is not left running
on the old binary: the supervisor respawns its children from
.IR /usr/bin/cfab ,
so an un\-restarted upgrade would run the next engine under the previous supervisor.
.PP
.B "apt remove"
stops the unit (correct: the binary is going away) and disables it;
.B "apt purge"
also removes
.IR /etc/default/cfab .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ip (8),
.BR nft (8),
.BR tc (8),
.BR vtysh (1),
.BR systemd\-run (1)
